<para id="X39400000R701MC" category="C" type-id="3008S" name-id="0659Z-00" from="200612">
<dtccode>P0171/25</dtccode>
<dtcname>Lean Malfunction (Bank1)</dtcname>
<dtccode>P0172/26</dtccode>
<dtcname>Rich Malfunction (Bank1)</dtcname>
<subpara id="X39400000R701MC_14" type-id="31" category="03" proc-id="RM0550J___00007JA00000">
<name>Circuit Description</name>
<content5 releasenbr="1">
<list1 type="nonmark">
<item>
<ptxt>Detected when the fuel correction amount by the air fuel ratio sensor (A/F sensor) exceeds a specified value. There are 2 types of air-fuel ratio correction: normal operation air-fuel ratio correction (hereafter air-fuel ratio F/B) and operating condition memory air-fuel ratio correction (hereafter air-fuel ratio F/B learning). Air-fuel ratio F/B is the air-fuel ratio correction used to maintain the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio; the engine control computer receives the signal from the air fuel ratio sensor indicating whether the current air-fuel ratio is richer (hereafter rich) or leaner (hereafter lean) than the stoichiometric ratio, and decreases the injection volume when rich and increases it when lean. Air-fuel ratio F/B learning means that, when air-fuel ratio F/B is performed intermittently over a long period, the F/B learning target value changes to match individual engine differences (caused by aging, changes in operating environment, etc.). When both the air-fuel ratio F/B value and the air-fuel ratio F/B learning value exceed a specified value toward lean or rich, the engine control computer illuminates the check engine warning light.</ptxt>
</item>
</list1>
<table frame="all" colsep="1" rowsep="1">
<tgroup cols="3" colsep="1" rowsep="1">
<colspec colnum="01" colname="C01" colwidth="0.9583659     in"/>
